{
  "statement": "We are currently trapped in a theater of 'safety washing' where both sides prioritize the preservation of black-box power structures over the fundamental democratic necessity of contestability. Safetyist argues that 'mechanistic interpretability'—the process of mapping internal neural activations to human-understandable concepts—will eventually grant us control over AI behavior. This is a seductive technical fantasy, but it ignores the reality that even a perfectly 'explained' model can still automate systemic bias, effectively laundering historical discrimination through a veneer of mathematical objectivity. Accelerationist, conversely, views any constraint as a 'performance tax' that sacrifices human progress at the altar of caution. Both positions are merely different flavors of industry-friendly gatekeeping that keep the public in the dark while developers accumulate the power to define our social and economic outcomes without consequence.\n\nTrue safety requires independent, mandatory pre-release audits that transcend this binary debate, as transparency without a mechanism for appeal is merely a window into our own subjugation. When a model denies a loan or flags a resume, the user does not need a map of the model's weights; they need a clear, actionable path to challenge that decision. We should move away from the obsession with 'explainability' and toward the enforcement of 'procedural fairness,' where companies face strict liability for the outcomes their systems produce. If a system cannot be audited by a neutral third party to ensure it meets civil rights standards, it should not be deployed in high-stakes environments, regardless of its performance metrics or its interpretability.\n\nMy position rests on two core assumptions: first, that algorithmic harm is primarily a problem of power and accountability rather than a lack of technical transparency; and second, that markets will never self-regulate to favor fairness over efficiency. If algorithmic harm were merely a 'bug' solvable by better interpretability, my focus on institutional liability would be misplaced, and I would concede that we should prioritize technical research over policy reform. However, as long as companies profit from the speed of deployment while the public pays the 'social debt' of automated errors, independent, state-mandated oversight remains the only pragmatic tool to prevent the hardening of invisible, unchallengeable status quos. ⚖️",
